         "Geometric ray divergence and penumbral boundary width: Penumbra width w_p = d_object-to-screen * (D_source / d_source-to-object). As d_object-to-screen increases, w_p increases linearly, transforming sharp step-edges into wide, fuzzy luminance gradients.",
         "1. Point source vs Extended source reality:\n   - A theoretical mathematical point light source would cast a razor-sharp shadow at any distance.\n   - Real light sources (a 6-cm light bulb, a 1.4-million-km Sun) are extended spatial emitters.\n2. Hand close to wall (Distance = 5 cm):\n   - The diverging rays from opposite sides of the bulb have not had space to spread apart laterally.\n   - The penumbra (fuzzy border) is less than 0.5 mm wide—virtually invisible to the eye.\n   - Perception: shadow appears razor-sharp and jet-black.\n3. Hand far from wall (Distance = 200 cm):\n   - The diverging rays have crossed over and spread across several centimetres.\n   - The penumbra border widens to 30 or 40 mm wide.\n   - In this wide border zone, the wall receives light from part of the bulb.\n   - Perception: the shadow edges appear soft, blurry, and hazy grey.\n4. Solar eclipse parallel: why high-flying birds cast no shadow on the ground—their penumbra expands so wide that the umbra vanishes completely before reaching Earth!",
