         "Light does not travel instantaneously; it travels at an exact, invariant speed of 299,792,458 m/s in vacuum; in 1676, Danish astronomer Ole Romer first proved light has finite speed by observing timing delays in the eclipses of Jupiter's moon Io as Earth moved closer to or farther from Jupiter.",
         "Relativistic invariance of c and electrodynamic wave propagation: c = 1 / sqrt(mu_0 * epsilon_0). In Special Relativity, c is Lorentz-invariant across all inertial reference frames. In 1983, the CGPM fixed c exactly to define the SI metre.",
         "1. Ancient belief: Aristotle, Descartes, and early scholars believed light traveled instantaneously (infinite speed).\n2. Ole Romer's breakthrough (1676):\n   - Monitored Io, a moon orbiting Jupiter every 42.5 hours.\n   - Discovered that when Earth was moving away from Jupiter in its orbit, Io's eclipses occurred 16 minutes LATER than predicted!\n   - Romer realized Io was not late—the light carrying the image took 16 extra minutes to travel across the wider orbital gap of Earth's orbit!\n   - First historical proof: light has finite, measurable velocity (~220,000 km/s in Romer's early estimate).\n3. Terrestrial laboratory measurements (Fizeau and Foucault, 1849-1862):\n   - Used rotating toothed wheels and spinning mirrors across kilometres of Paris to measure c accurate to within 1%.\n4. Modern definition (1983):\n   - Speed of light in vacuum is defined EXACTLY as c = 299,792,458 m/s.\n   - Because c is invariant, science now uses the speed of light to define the exact length of 1 metre (distance light travels in 1/299,792,458 s).\n5. Conclude: c links space and time together into spacetime.",
