         "The human eye is a biological camera: 1. The transparent Cornea and Crystalline Lens act as optical lenses focusing light rays; 2. The Pupil acts as an adjustable aperture controlling light entry (like a pinhole); 3. The Retina acts as the light-sensitive screen at the back, containing 130 million photoreceptor cells that convert the inverted optical image into electrical nerve impulses sent to the brain.",
         "Bio-optical refractive imaging and retinal transduction: dual refractive surfaces (cornea n approx 1.376, lens n approx 1.406) provide ~60 dioptres of convergent power, focusing real inverted images onto the retina. Iris sphincter muscles modulate entrance pupil diameter (2-8 mm).",
         "1. Camera analogy mapping:\n   - Pinhole / Aperture = Pupil (dilates in dark, constricts in bright light).\n   - Glass lens = Cornea (fixed outer lens) + Crystalline Lens (flexible inner lens adjusted by ciliary muscles to focus near vs far).\n   - Dark box interior = Sclera and Choroid (pigmented dark layer preventing internal light reflections).\n   - Projection Screen = Retina (neural layer packed with 120 million rod cells for night vision and 6 million cone cells for color vision).\n2. The optical ray path:\n   - Light reflects off a tree and enters the cornea.\n   - Passes through the aqueous humor and pupil opening.\n   - Crystalline lens refracts light rays, bending them to a sharp focus.\n   - Image on retina: 100% REAL, INVERTED (upside down), and diminished in size!\n3. The brain's magic:\n   - Photoreceptors convert photons into action potential nerve impulses.\n   - The optic nerve transmits 10 million bits of data per second to the visual cortex in the brain.\n   - The brain instantly flips the inverted retinal image right-side up, combining two eyes into seamless 3D stereoscopic vision.\n4. Conclude: human vision unites physics, optics, biology, and neuroscience.",
