         "Electromagnetic polarization and Brewster's angle: specular reflection off a dielectric interface at Brewster's angle theta_B = arctan(n) induces 100% s-polarization (electric field vector parallel to the surface plane). Polyvinyl alcohol-iodine stretched polymers in polarized filters absorb s-polarized glare via anisotropic conduction.",
         "1. Unpolarized light physics:\n   - Light emitted by the Sun or lightbulbs is unpolarized: its electric field oscillates in every possible perpendicular direction (up-down, left-right, diagonally).\n2. Glare formation on horizontal surfaces:\n   - When unpolarized sunbeams strike a flat asphalt road, puddle, car hood, or lake, the horizontal surface filters the light.\n   - The reflected rays are forced to oscillate primarily in ONE horizontal plane (s-polarization).\n   - This concentrated horizontal wave is what human eyes perceive as blinding, washed-out 'glare' that hides fish under water and obscures wet roads.\n3. Ordinary sunglasses failure: dark tinted glasses have dark dye that absorbs 70% of ALL light equally—dimming both the glare and the scenery, leaving vision murky.\n4. Polarized sunglasses mechanism:\n   - The plastic lens is stretched during manufacturing to align long-chain polyvinyl alcohol polymer molecules in parallel horizontal rows.\n   - These molecular rows absorb horizontally vibrating light waves completely!\n   - Only vertically vibrating light waves slip through the vertical gaps.\n5. Visual triumph: 100% of blinding horizontal glare is wiped out instantly; fishermen can see straight through the lake water to spot fish swimming beneath, and drivers see clear road markings without squinting.",
